 strain

Frank won 33 tickets playing 'whack a mole' and 9 tickets playing 'skee ball'. So, the total number of tickets Frank won is

\begin{align*}
    \text{Total Tickets} &= \text{Tickets from Whack a Mole} + \text{Tickets from Skee Ball} \\
                         &= 33 + 9 \\
                         &= \boxed{42}
\end{align*}

Each candy costs 6 tickets, so the number of candies Frank can buy is the quotient of the total number of tickets and the cost per candy.

\begin{align*}
    \text{Number of Candies} &= \frac{\text{Total Tickets}}{\text{Cost Per Ticket}} \\
                              &= \frac{42}{6} \\
                              &= \boxed{7}
\end{align*}

So, Frank can buy \boxed{7} candies.

Alternatively, you can also calculate how many groups of 6 candies can be bought with 42 tickets by simply dividing the total tickets by 6:

\begin{align*}
    \text{Groups of 6 Candies} &= \frac{\text{Total Tickets}}{6} \\
                                 &= \frac{42}{6} \\
                                 &= 7
\end{align*}

And since each group represents 6 candies, Frank can buy \boxed{7} candies.

